Morning,

I need help with something that sounds simple but probably is not.

Collections wants to reduce unnecessary contact by identifying customers who =
usually restore the account without intervention. Please start with the decis=
ion we need to make, then show whether the available evidence is strong enoug=
h to support it.

The questions I would ask in the room are:
1. What would we expect to see if balance recovery and payment timing identif=
y self-cure?
2. What evidence would instead support that past contact creates the apparent=
 cure?
3. How do we rule out that repeat NSF customers need a different treatment?

This has returned because the previous answer was useful but not strong enoug=
h to become a standing rule. Use April 2025 as the new evidence point rather =
than recycling the earlier conclusion. Please work towards which accounts can=
 receive a light-touch reminder and which need an agent. A useful output woul=
d be a control view with the exceptions, owner and reason each item was inclu=
ded.

Use collections cases, promises to pay and recovery payments; transaction-lev=
el timestamps, channels, amounts, statuses and error context; customer contac=
ts, complaints and suggestions; debit-order mandates, collection dates, suspe=
nsions and cancellations. One caution: Observed cure after contact is not pro=
of that the contact caused it.

